## 1. What was reduced, and what is being claimed

* **Parent mechanism** (`parent.yaml`, supplied, unmodified): Cantera-3.1-format USC-II,
  **111 species / 784 reactions**, NASA-7 thermodynamics, mixture-averaged transport,
  elements C-H-O-N-Ar, no low-temperature (NTC) chemistry.
* **Claim of this work**: a family of *skeletal* mechanisms that are strict species/reaction
  subsets of the parent, with a quantified accuracy-versus-size trade-off, and an
  explicit statement of accuracy against **measurements** (shock-tube ignition delays and
  laminar burning velocities) as distinct from fidelity to the parent mechanism.
* **Not claimed**: global optimality, validity outside the stated domain, or that species
  removal is the only / best compression route.

Because every reduced mechanism is a byte-level subset of the parent (see §5.1), it
inherits the parent's thermochemistry, transport data and rate parameters exactly.  Any
disagreement with experiment is therefore either inherited from USC-II or produced by the
removal itself — never by re-fitting.

## 2. Application domain (declared before the reduction was run)

| axis | range used for reduction and validation |
|---|---|
| fuels | H2, CO/H2 syngas, CH4, CH3OH, C2H2, C2H4, C2H6, C3H6, C3H8, n-/i-C4H10, C4H81, C4H6, CH3CHO, C6H6, C6H5CH3 |
| equivalence ratio | 0.5 – 2.0 (reduction control), 0.2 – 2.2 (experimental coverage) |
| pressure | 1 – 30 atm (reduction control); one out-of-domain check at 99 – 197 atm |
| temperature | 900 – 1800 K (ignition), 298 – 400 K unburned (flames) |
| dilution | Ar (x=0.88) shock-tube style, N2 (x=0.79) "air-side" style, true air for flames |
| observables | ignition delay (several diagnostic definitions), laminar burning velocity, adiabatic equilibrium temperature |

Omitted on purpose: low-temperature/RCM conditions below ~900 K, NOx/soot chemistry,
turbulent or non-premixed configurations, and any fuel outside the parent's species set
(ethanol, ammonia, higher hydrocarbons).  The parent mechanism contains no low-temperature
peroxy chemistry, so extension there is not possible by removal.

## 3. Sources used, and what was unavailable

* **FFCM-2 / Stanford Fundamental Combustion Property Database (SFCPD)** —
  `https://web.stanford.edu/group/haiwanglab/FFCM2/docs/ExperimentalData/SFCPD/`.
  Reachable.  The public validation table (`validation_table.json`, 1192 targets) was
  downloaded, and then **all 1067 individual validation pages** were downloaded, giving
  per-target temperature, pressure, equivalence ratio, mixture composition, curated
  experimental value **with uncertainty**, experimental method and the underlying
  references.  This is the primary external-validation source used here.
* **ReSpecTh** (`https://respecth.elte.hu/`) — the site is now a single-page application
  and the underlying data require registration; **not used directly**.
* **SciExpeM ReSpecTh-format XML archive on Zenodo** — used instead: 1094 experiment files
  were enumerated and downloaded (records listed in `raw/sciexpm/zenodo_all.json`,
  dataset DOIs in `raw/sciexpm/experiments_doi.txt`).  Parsed with a purpose-written
  ReSpecTh reader (`src/respecth.py`).
* **ChemKED database** (`https://github.com/pr-omethe-us/ChemKED-database`) — downloaded
  and inspected; its content overlapped the Zenodo archive and was not used further.
* **pyMARS** (`https://github.com/Niemeyer-Research-Group/pyMARS`) — downloaded and used as
  a line-level reference for the DRGEP implementation (`raw/pymars/`).

Nothing was assumed about a database's fuel range matching USC-II's: every candidate
record was filtered by "all species present in the parent species list".

## 4. Data-quality findings that changed the validation design

These were found by checking the archived record against its own internal consistency and
against FFCM-2.  All are documented in code (`src/build_ffcm2_sets.py`,
`src/annotate_flame_subset.py`) and reproducible.

1. **Syngas laminar-burning-velocity records have a wrong mixture.**  In the SciExpeM
   syngas LBV files the `equivalence ratio` column equals the **fuel/O2 molar ratio**
   (= 2x the stoichiometric-normalised value for a 50/50 H2/CO blend), and the
   composition column was generated from that label
   (X_O2 = (X_H2+X_CO)/phi_field, X_N2 = 3.76 X_O2, exact on every row).  The composition
   therefore describes a mixture twice as lean as the experiment.  Evidence:
   FFCM-2's curated target `fls_H2+CO_5_4` lists
   `14.81% H2 - 14.81% CO - 14.79% O2 - 55.60% N2` at phi = 1.0 (the composition our
   archive file labels phi = 2.0), and its consensus experimental value is
   **108.6 ± 6.6 cm/s**, which brackets the archive's own S_L values on the rows labelled
   phi = 1.0 (94.8 – 127.3 cm/s across nine series of the same paper).  The syngas LBV
   compositions used here were therefore **rebuilt from the phi field**.
2. **The same artefact does not affect the syngas ignition records** — for all 1307
   archived ignition records the stated equivalence ratio agrees with the one recomputed
   from the composition to <2% (median 0.02%).
3. **Diagnostic definitions differ between ignition datasets.**  All 1307 archived ignition
   records are shock-tube measurements, but the reported observable is max d[OH]/dt (896),
   max d[CH]/dt (266), peak [OH] (137) or an OH-concentration threshold (8).  A single
   model diagnostic (max dT/dt) would have mixed mechanism error with
   diagnostic-definition error, so every model ignition evaluation returns
   tau_T, tau_OH, tau_CH, tau_maxOH, tau_maxCH and tau_p from one trajectory
   (`src/obs2.py`) and each record is compared with the matching model diagnostic.
4. **Datasets that cannot be simulated with this mechanism were dropped, not approximated.**
   1784 flame records involve NH3 and 92 involve He as a diluent; neither is a USC-II
   species.  Their exclusion is why C2H6, C3H8 and CH3OH have no raw-archive flame
   coverage here — all their archived LBV datasets in this archive are NH3-blended.
   FFCM-2 does provide NH3-free curated targets for those fuels, and those are used.
5. **Repeated points from one series are not independent studies.**  Where several
   datasets measure the same mixture (e.g. nine syngas LBV series at the same
   composition), the spread between them (95 – 127 cm/s at phi = 1.0) is reported rather
   than treated as independent evidence of model error.

6. **Seven pairs of archive files contain byte-identical data with contradictory unit
   declarations, and one whole benzene series declares values that are impossible as
   milliseconds.**  `src/archive_unit_audit.py` compares every pair of files in the
   archive: seven pairs have identical data points, identical composition and identical
   pressure but declare the ignition delay as `ms` in one copy and `us` in the other
   (e.g. `zenodo.4733738` / `zenodo.5702659`, both Marchal et al., both shock tube).  Only
   the microsecond reading is physically possible: the other files of the same benzene
   series carry values of 688-1978 under the `ms` declaration, i.e. up to 2 s behind a
   reflected shock, where the test time is 1-10 ms.  All seven pairs and eleven further
   files were corrected as follows (`src/unitfix_idt.py`): the `ms` member of a pair is
   dropped (its twin is kept), and a file that declares `ms` while containing a value
   above 50 ms is re-read as microseconds.  The corrected dataset is
   `results/val_idt_unitfix.json` (68 duplicate records dropped, 59 values re-read); the
   uncorrected comparison is kept as `results/exp_tables_idt.md`.  Effect: the archived
   benzene family median falls from ln = -6.66 to -0.46 for the parent, and the largest
   remaining benzene deviation falls from 9.27 to 2.36 log units (sensitivity in 7.6).

## 5. Reduction method

### 5.1 Text-level subsetting (`src/mech_text.py`)

A reduced mechanism is produced by deleting species from the phase `species:` list, their
`thermo`/`transport` blocks, every reaction whose participating species set (reactants,
products and explicit third-body colliders) is not fully retained, third-body efficiency
entries for deleted species, and by adding `duplicate: true` where two reactions become
textually identical.  Everything kept is copied **byte-identically** from the parent.
Round-trip verification: regenerating the parent with the full species set reproduces all
111 species and 784 reactions with identical equation lists and a maximum relative
difference of 0 in the forward rate constants and in h/RT.

Consequences: elemental conservation and thermodynamic consistency are structural (no
parameter is touched), any reduced mechanism is exactly reproducible from the parent plus
a species list, and the reduction cannot produce a physically inconsistent mechanism.

### 5.2 Elimination order: measured one-at-a-time removal impact

Ordering came from a screening stage: each candidate species is removed alone and the
resulting mechanism is scored on a 52-condition screening set
(every fuel family of the development matrix: 18 families at 1400 K in Ar at 10 and 30 atm
and 16 conditions at 1600 K in N2 at 1 atm), together with the 54 equilibrium states.  The
score is `max(|ln(tau/tau_parent)|)` over that set together with the maximum deviation of
the adiabatic equilibrium temperature.  This is a screening heuristic, not an error proof:
the effects of removing several species are not additive, which is why it only sets the
*order*.

### 5.3 Error-controlled greedy backward elimination (`src/pipeline.py`)

Species are removed in ascending screening-impact order, in batches, and a batch is
accepted **only** if the resulting mechanism satisfies all of, on the development matrix:

* `max |ln(tau/tau_parent)| <= 0.60` (worst case ~1.8x),
* `rms ln(tau/tau_parent) <= 0.20`,
* `max |Tad - Tad_parent| <= 20 K` over 54 equilibrium states.

Rejected batches are halved down to a single species, and species that cannot be removed
alone are recorded as required.  Three passes are made so that species rejected early can
be retried later.  A milestone mechanism is written at every acceptance, so the whole
size/accuracy curve comes out of one run and the deliverable can be chosen *after* the
curve is known instead of fixing a size beforehand.

**Development matrix.**  18 fuel families (H2, CO/H2, CH4, CH3OH, C2H2, C2H4, C2H6, C3H6,
C3H8, n-/i-C4H10, C4H81, C4H82, C4H6, C6H6, C6H5CH3, CH3CHO, CH3COCH3) x {10 atm Ar phi=1,
30 atm Ar phi=0.5} x {1100 K, 1400 K}, plus eight families at 1 atm N2 phi=1,2 and
1200/1600 K: **104 ignition conditions**, all of them synthetic parent-mechanism conditions
(no experimental value is used to control the reduction).  Ignition delays are compared
through the same multi-diagnostic solver used for the experimental comparison, and the
metric is the worst and the RMS over the 104 conditions.

**Protected species.**  N2, Ar, He, O2, OH, H, O on the evidence of the screening stage
(single removal of H, O, OH already produces a log-error of 8.40, 5.55 and non-ignition
respectively), plus **every fuel species of the declared application domain**, so that no
mechanism of the family can lose the ability to represent a fuel that the validation data
cover.

**A documented false start.**  The first elimination run protected only the fuel species of
the *original* development matrix, which did not contain CH3CHO, CH3COCH3 or C4H82.  It
removed CH3CHO and CH3COCH3 (they are nearly invisible to the remaining families'
ignition delays) and thereby produced a mechanism that cannot represent two fuels that the
declared domain and the validation data contain.  That run is kept as
`results/elimination_log.json` (v1) and was discarded; the domain-fuel protection above was
added and the run repeated (`results/elimination_log2.json`).  This is exactly the kind of
error that a "smaller is better" criterion hides, so it is reported rather than deleted.

### 5.4 Alternatives considered and why they were not used as the primary method

* **DRGEP (path-propagated directed relation graph)** was implemented (`src/drgep.py`) and
  checked line-by-line against pyMARS.  With the sampled ignition states it has **no
  discriminative power**: at epsilon = 0.9 it still retains 108/111 species, and lowering
  epsilon to 0.01 changes nothing.  Reordering the aggregation (propagate per state, then
  take the maximum, which is pyMARS' order) only moves the count from 93 to 88 species
  above 0.9 for a single case (`src/drgep_diag.py`).  The reason is structural: the direct
  coefficient r_AB is close to 1 whenever species A participates in only a few reactions,
  which is true for most minor species.  DRGEP was therefore demoted to a diagnostic and
  is not used for the elimination order.
* **DRG (one-hop) coefficients** were computed as a by-product; they have more dynamic
  range than the DRGEP score but still almost no separation (score quantiles over the 111
  species: 1 % -> 0.00, 5 % -> 0.33, 10 % -> 1.00, median -> 1.00, and every species above
  the 10th percentile sits at 1.0).  Thresholding them keeps 108 species at
  epsilon = 0.001-0.3, 106 at 0.5, 105 at 0.9 and 104 at 0.99, i.e. a "graph +
  threshold" reduction of this mechanism under a max-over-states aggregation removes at
  most 7 of 111 species.  They were not used as the criterion, both for that reason and
  because a one-hop coefficient has no error semantics; the measured single-removal impact
  was preferred as it is defined directly in terms of the observables of interest.
* **State-specific DRGEP + union** is the usual repair for the aggregation problem, but it
  reduces a *single* state only: for CH4 10 atm 1300 K the best single-state skeleton at
  epsilon = 0.9 is 88 species (measured, `src/drgep_diag.py`), and a mechanism that must be
  valid over 104 diverse conditions would have to be the union of such skeletons.  A union
  of 88-species skeletons is not a reduction.  This is why the accuracy control is applied
  to the multi-condition observable error directly.
* **QSSA / time-scale-based reduction** would give smaller mechanisms but introduces
  algebraic constraints and steady-state species, i.e. it changes the mathematical
  structure of the model and is much harder to validate at the level of a strict subset.
  Skeletal removal was preferred because every claim stays checkable.
* **Reaction-flux pruning** (removing reactions whose integrated flux is negligible while
  keeping all species) was not run: species removal already deletes the reactions that
  involve the removed species, and the remaining USC-II reactions are individually
  load-bearing for the conditions validated here.

## 6. Verification of the custom numerics

* **Text surgery**: round-trip regeneration of the parent reproduces 111 species / 784
  reactions, identical equations, zero difference in forward rate constants and h/RT.
* **Ignition-delay implementation**: the multi-diagnostic solver (`src/obs2.py`) is
  compared against the earlier single-diagnostic implementation and against changes of
  the sampling grid and solver tolerance (`src/obs2_check.py`).
* **Event location (found defect, fixed)**: the first version of the solver bracketed the
  ignition event with a geometric time sequence (factor 8) and then sampled 120 points in
  that bracket.  Its time resolution was therefore `(t_cur - t_prev)/119`, i.e. 1-6 % of
  the reported delay, and three chemically different mixtures
  (CH3CHO/Ar 10 atm 1100 K, CH3CHO/N2 1 atm 1200 K, C4H82/N2 1 atm 1200 K) returned the
  *identical* value 6.414818e-3 s because they fell on the same grid point.  That
  quantisation was of the same order as the fidelity differences the reduction was
  supposed to measure.  The solver now performs `levels + 1` refinement passes, each
  narrowing the bracket to the union of the grid cells around *all* diagnostic maxima; the
  same three cases now give 6.4168e-3, 6.5214e-3 and 6.5138e-3 s, and refining further
  (npts 120 -> 400) changes the result by 0.06 %, 0.27 % and 0.15 %.  The final grid
  spacing is reported with every result.
* **Flame-speed implementation**: Cantera's `FreeFlame` solver with a stated refinement
  criterion and `max_grid_points`; convergence is recorded per point, and the sensitivity
  of S_L to the refinement level is measured rather than assumed.  Five mixtures (H2/Ar,
  CH4/air, C2H4/air, C6H6/air, CO+H2/air, spanning S_L = 0.31-2.24 m/s) were solved at three
  refinement levels for the parent, the 103-species and the 68-species mechanism
  (`results/flame_grid_*.json`, remote job 7957969).  The production (`medium`) level differs
  from the finest level by 0.04-2.8 % (median 1.8 %), the coarse level by 0.4-5.4 %; the
  parent and the 103-species mechanism return *identical* values at every level
  (e.g. 2.2403 / 2.2173 / 2.2013 m/s for H2), while the 68-species C6H6 value differs from
  the parent by -18.6 % at the medium level.  The discretisation error is therefore smaller
  than every mechanism difference the flame criterion acts on (6.7 % and 15.4 % for the
  91- and 88-species mechanisms), but comparable to the <= 1.1 % differences seen at 103
  species; those are reported as "no measurable change at this resolution".
* **Equilibrium**: Cantera `equilibrate('HP')` on identical initial states for parent and
  reduced mechanisms; this is the thermodynamic-consistency check.
* **Steady-state / physical sanity**: all reported ignition delays correspond to a
  temperature rise of more than 400 K; non-igniting cases are counted and reported instead
  of being silently dropped.

## 7. Results

### 7.0 Decision rule, fixed before the experimental comparison was run

The deliverable is not "the smallest mechanism".  The following rule was written down
before the validation job was submitted, so that the choice is not made by looking at the
experimental numbers and picking the winner:

1. **Hard requirements** (must hold on the *disjoint* confirmation matrix, i.e. on the
   conditions never used inside the reduction loop): `max |ln(tau/tau_parent)| <= 0.35`,
   `rms <= 0.12`, `max |Tad - Tad_parent| <= 20 K`.
2. **Additional hard requirement**: on the archetype flame curve (one mixture per fuel
   family, computed with the production refinement settings) the reduced mechanism must
   stay within 10 % of the parent laminar burning velocity in every family.
3. **Selection**: among the mechanisms that pass 1 and 2, take the one with the fewest
   species.
4. **Experimental check (not a selection criterion)**: the chosen mechanism's agreement
   with the FFCM-2/SFCPD and SciExpeM/ReSpecTh measurements is reported next to the
   parent's, per fuel family and per source, with the number of independent studies.  The
   comparison is made on the unit-audited dataset (section 4, item 6): the `ms`/`us`
   duplicate pairs are de-duplicated and physically impossible millisecond values are
   re-read as microseconds.  This correction is applied to every mechanism identically, so
   it cannot favour one of them, and the uncorrected tables are kept next to the corrected
   ones.  If
   the reduction makes the agreement with experiment *materially worse* than the parent
   (median |ln(model/exp)| degraded by more than 0.05 in log units, or any family's worst
   case doubled), that is reported as a failure of the trade-off even if 1-3 passed.

Reason for separating 3 and 4: fidelity to the parent and accuracy against experiment are
different statements.  A reduced mechanism can agree with experiment better than the parent
by accident (two errors cancelling) and it can be perfectly faithful to the parent while
the parent itself is wrong.  The reduction is only allowed to claim the first.

### 7.1 The two reduction paths that were kept, and the one that was discarded

The elimination is a greedy search, so the path (and therefore the frontier reached) depends
on the order in which species are offered for removal and on the tolerance.  Three runs were
made with the same screening order, the same development matrix and the same acceptance
test, differing only in the tolerance and in the protection set:

| run | tolerance (max / rms) | protection | final size | kept as |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| v1 (discarded) | 0.30 / 0.10 | 15 dev fuels only | 73 species | `results/elimination_log.json` — invalid, removed two domain fuels |
| v2 (`loose`) | 0.60 / 0.20 | 15 dev fuels + domain fuels | 61 species | `results/elimination_log2.json`, `mech2/` |
| v3 (`tight`) | 0.30 / 0.10 | 15 dev fuels + domain fuels | 68 species | `results/elimination_log3.json`, `mech3/` |

The v3 path reaches a strictly better frontier than v2 (68 species at 20 % worst-case
development error, where v2 needed 87 species for 56 %, and its 61-species end is at 46 %).
This is a property of greedy elimination, not of the physics: the order in which species are
proposed changes which *combinations* are ever tested.  Both paths are therefore reported.  The
pre-registered rule rejects both ends, and the aggressive rung that is delivered is the
v3 68-species mechanism because it dominates the v2 61-species one on every fidelity
measure (confirmation max / rms 1.678 / 0.118 against 2.079 / 0.252, and the same 0.0 K
equilibrium error); v2's 61-species end is kept in `mech2/` as evidence that the path,
not the tolerance, is what limits the reachable frontier.

