### 7.6 What the experimental comparison says about the low-temperature regime

The pre-registered confirmation tolerance (max |ln| <= 0.35 over all T) is violated by every
mechanism below 103 species, and the violating cases are all aromatic/Ar, 900-1100 K,
10-30 atm.  Two independent questions follow: does the experimental record constrain that
regime at all, and does the parent mechanism behave any better there than the reduced ones?

**Temperatures at which the archived measurements actually exist** (unit-audited set):
900-1000 K: 44 usable points, all H2 (37) and C2H4 (7); 1000-1100 K: 120 usable points
(H2, C2H4, C2H2, CH4, CH3OH, C3H8, C4H81); 1100-2300 K: 987 igniting points over 18 fuel
families.  A further 155 syngas (CO+H2, 1000-1500 K, Ar-diluted) and 248 H2 points do not
produce any ignition event within the 20 s reactor limit for *any* mechanism, including the
parent; they are reported as censored, not as failures of a particular reduction.
The aromatic shock-tube data that do exist are at 2.4-9.1 atm, not at the 10-30 atm of the
worst confirmation cases, and no experimental target in either source is near
C6H6/Ar 30 atm 900 K.  **The extreme errors of section 7.3 therefore have no external
measurement behind them**: they are statements about agreement with the parent mechanism,
not about accuracy against experiment.

**Agreement with the measurements that do exist in the low-temperature bands** (median
|ln(model/exp)|, unit-audited, all mechanisms):

| T band | n | parent | 103 sp | 88 sp | 68 sp |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 900-1000 K | 44 | 0.170 | 0.170 | 0.170 | 0.152 |
| 1000-1100 K | 120 | 0.371 | 0.373 | 0.373 | 0.486 |
| 1100-1300 K | 402 | 0.467 | 0.467 | 0.468 | 0.514 |
| 1300-1500 K | 355 | 0.400 | 0.400 | 0.407 | 0.477 |
| 1500-2300 K | 230 | 0.549 | 0.551 | 0.549 | 0.565 |

The parent mechanism is not better in the low-temperature bands than it is at high
temperature; if anything the median deviation is smallest there (0.17 at 900-1000 K), which
is a statement about which datasets exist in that band (H2 and C2H4, two well-studied
fuels), not evidence that USC-II is accurate for aromatics at 900 K.  The conclusion drawn
here is deliberately narrow: **within the temperature range that has experimental coverage,
the 103- and 88-species mechanisms are indistinguishable from the parent against the
measurements; the extra error of the smaller mechanisms sits in a low-temperature aromatic
corner that the archived measurements do not reach.**

