   the attribution measurement of section 7.4 (so it is explicitly not a blind, pre-registered
   choice).  Measured on the same matrices it is better than the 91-species milestone on every
   axis (confirmation max / rms 0.487 / 0.040 against 0.853 / 0.060; identical 6.7 % worst
   archetype flame deviation) and better than the 88-species mechanism by 8.7 percentage
   points on the binding C2H2 flame deviation.  It still fails the pre-registered
   confirmation-maximum criterion, in the same unvalidated 900-1100 K aromatic corner.
3. `mech3/reduced_final.yaml` — 68 species / 473 reactions (39 % fewer).  Ignition delays
   within 41 % (rms 6.8 %) above 1100 K and within 19 % for flames, but the low-temperature
   aromatic corner is lost (up to a factor 5.4 at C6H6/Ar 30 atm 900 K) and benzene and
   ethylene agreement with experiment degrades by 0.3-0.4 log units.  This is the
   "aggressive" mechanism of the trade-off, not a drop-in replacement.

The choice of 103 over the smaller mechanisms is the choice of the pre-registered rule and
is reported as such; the measured consequence of overriding it is in the table above.  What would change
this decision: (i) a measurement in the 900-1100 K, 10-30 atm aromatic corner, which would
either confirm or remove the binding constraint; (ii) evidence about the C2H2
flame-speed sensitivity of the 88-species mechanism — this was measured, and it promoted the
89-species re-addition variant into the delivered set (section 7.4); (iii) a different weighting of the
two observables, since the ignition and flame criteria select different mechanisms.

## 8. Limitations and coverage gaps

* **Reactor model.** All ignition delays are computed in an adiabatic, constant-volume
  homogeneous reactor starting from the post-reflected-shock state.  Real shock tubes have
  boundary-layer and non-ideal-pressure effects, and the ignition-delay datasets that come
  from rapid-compression machines or flow reactors are constant-pressure.  No constant-
  pressure ignition calculation was run.
* **Flame model.** One-dimensional, freely propagating, adiabatic, mixture-averaged
  transport with Cantera's `FreeFlame`.  No multicomponent/Soret transport, no stretch, no
  burner-stabilised configurations; the grid-dependence is measured separately.
* **Diagnostic definitions.** Experimental ignition-delay definitions differ (max d[OH]/dt,
  max d[CH]/dt, peak [OH], threshold crossing, pressure rise, onset extrapolations).  The
  archive states a definition per experiment and a mapping to the closest model diagnostic
  is used (`src/targets.py`); the spread over all model diagnostics is reported as a band
  rather than hidden.  Onset definitions ("the extrapolated onset of CH* emission") are only
  approximated by the corresponding peak diagnostic.
* **Uncertainties.** FFCM-2/SFCPD targets carry curated uncertainties; the SciExpeM/ReSpecTh
  archive used here mostly does not (`exp_rel_unc = None` for ~97 % of the ignition points
  and ~68 % of the flame points).  Where no uncertainty exists, deviations are reported in
  log units without a "within uncertainty" claim, and repeated points from one publication
  are counted as one study.
* **Out-of-domain checks.** Aromatic (C6H6, C6H5CH3) and C2H2 ignition experiments reach
  equivalence ratios up to ~20 and pressures up to 60 atm; the validation sets are reported
  per family so that these extremes are visible rather than averaged away.
* **Coverage holes.** (i) Single-fuel CH4 high-pressure ignition data in the archive are
  all 99-197 atm, above the reduction domain; they are reported as an out-of-domain check
  rather than as validation.  (ii) The archived laminar-burning-velocity files for C2H6,
  C3H8 and CH3OH contain ammonia or helium and cannot be simulated with USC-II, so those
  families' flame validation comes from FFCM-2 only.  (iii) ReSpecTh's own site requires
  registration and was not used; the Zenodo SciExpeM archive was used instead.
* **Thermodynamic closure.** Removing a species removes its contribution to the third-body
  mixture; the reduced mechanisms keep H2O and CO2 (their removal alone changes Tad by 926 K
  and 1443 K) and all H/O/OH radical chemistry that the screening flagged.  No re-fitting of
  any rate or thermo parameter was performed, so the reduced mechanisms inherit USC-II's own
  errors; agreement with experiment is therefore bounded by the parent's agreement.
* **The aromatic corner is not validated.**  The conditions that set the confirmation
  tolerance (C6H6/C6H5CH3, 10-30 atm, 900-1100 K) have no counterpart in either experimental
  source used here (the archived aromatic measurements are at 2.4-9.1 atm and mostly above
  1200 K).  The reduction error reported for them is a fidelity-to-parent statement only.
* **Parent-mechanism errors dominate the largest disagreements.**  Methanol (+1.53 log
  units), acetylene (+1.43), acetaldehyde (+0.90) and ethane (-0.65) medians are the same
  before and after the reduction; no amount of species removal changes them, and no
  re-fitting was attempted (it would break the "strict subset" property that makes every
  claim here checkable).
* **Non-ignition is censoring, not a measurement of zero reactivity.**  461 of the 1612
  ignition targets show no ignition event within 20 s in the adiabatic constant-volume
  reactor; 403 of them are H2 or CO/H2 in argon, where the parent predicts delays longer
  than any shock-tube test time.  These points are excluded from the medians (they have no
  finite ratio) but counted in every table.
* **The archive contains at least one more unit-level inconsistency of the same kind.**
  `src/archive_unit_audit.py` flags 17 files (out of 1094) as either duplicate-with-conflicting
  units or physically impossible milliseconds; the audit only checks the first data group of
  each file and only compares files that are byte-identical in their data points, so a
  differently written duplicate would not be caught.  Any future use of this archive should
  run the audit and treat the aromatic/toluene/1-pentanol millisecond series as suspect.
* **Grid dependence of the flame speed** is measured, not assumed, in
  `results/flame_grid_*.json`: the production (`medium`) refinement level is compared with a
  coarser and a finer level on five families; the reported mechanism-to-mechanism differences
  of section 7.4 are 6-19 % while the discretisation error is reported separately in section 9.
* **No constant-pressure and no RCM-style calculation was run**, so the datasets whose
  apparatus is a rapid-compression machine or a flow reactor are compared against a
  constant-volume trajectory.  The archive's `apparatus` field is reported per dataset but
  was not used to switch the reactor model.

